{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786480214,"nonce":"aX4oKN3DcXfXlWxmZQvcl/Ii9dc=","target":"3de73acd25da3a3897cca08b25ee55e17280948b9ea217785b120cb41e8891ae","message":"**What is wrong.** `Idempotency-Key` is declared on `createApiToken` and `revokeApiToken` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (`parameters/IdempotencyKeyHeader`), and the contract states what it means: *\"Replaying the same key with the same body within the retention window returns the original result rather than acting twice; replaying it with a different body is a `409`.\"*\n\n**Nothing in oto reads the header.** `internal/platform/httpx/middleware` allows it through CORS, and that is the only mention of it in the entire process.\n\n**What that costs.** A client that retries a create — a dropped response, a proxy timeout, a user double-clicking \"generate\" — gets a **SECOND LIVE CREDENTIAL**, and has no way to tell that outcome from the first attempt having worked. The secret of the extra token is returned to a request whose response may never have arrived, so an orphaned live credential can exist that nobody knows the value of and nobody knows to revoke.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the exact failure an idempotency key exists to prevent, occurring on the one endpoint in oto that hands out credentials. It is a security defect, not merely a correctness one: the blast radius is a live API token outside anyone's inventory. The contract advertising the protection makes it worse, because a careful client will retry *believing* it is safe.\n\n**Where.** `internal/identity/api/tokens.go` (`createAPIToken`, `revokeAPIToken`), `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (`parameters/IdempotencyKeyHeader`), `internal/platform/httpx/middleware`.\n\n**Pinned by** the skipped `TestBUG_ARetriedCreateApiTokenWithTheSameIdempotencyKeyMintsASecondCredential` in `internal/identity/api/identity_contract_test.go:938`.\n\n**Done when.** A retried create with the same key and body replays the original 201 rather than minting a second credential; the same key with a different body is a 409; the same holds for `revokeApiToken`; and the pin is unskipped. Note the ack/snooze paths already mint keys per user gesture — check whether they honour them, or whether this hole is wider than the two token endpoints.","files":null}]}